WEBVTT

01-01
00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:01.865
Welcome to week four.

01-02
00:00:02.045 --> 00:00:10.331
Before we begin solid equilibria and adsorption, let us answer the
questions that came from your exit tickets and feedback.

01-03
00:00:10.511 --> 00:00:19.642
We will explain what consistency scores mean, read the Gibbs energy
graph, and distinguish phase compositions from phase amounts.

01-04
00:00:19.822 --> 00:00:23.214
Keep a calculator and your notebook beside you.

01-05
00:00:23.394 --> 00:00:31.458
Later in this opening, you will pause for four minutes and write three
short answers, labelled R one through R three.

01-06
00:00:31.638 --> 00:00:35.819
This video opening replaces the separate opening reading.

01-07
00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:38.303
You do not need to repeat both.

01-08
00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:44.457
It also replaces the formerly required H exercises, which are now
optional.

01-09
00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:53.133
After this review, continue with worksheet sections A through G, then
section I and the existing Session four form.

01-10
00:00:53.313 --> 00:00:57.452
There is no additional submission for these opening questions.

02-01
00:00:59.982 --> 00:01:04.227
Three questions sound similar, but require different evidence.

02-02
00:01:04.407 --> 00:01:09.577
First, do the activity data satisfy a thermodynamic relationship?

02-03
00:01:09.757 --> 00:01:18.205
For that question, examine Gibbs-Duhem diagnostics and state the
experimental path, assumptions and numerical method.

02-04
00:01:18.385 --> 00:01:23.887
Second, can a chosen model reproduce the observed vapor-liquid
equilibrium?

02-05
00:01:24.067 --> 00:01:30.317
Compare calculated and observed pressure and vapor composition, using
a specified fit.

02-06
00:01:30.497 --> 00:01:33.335
Third, is a proposed phase state stable?

02-07
00:01:33.515 --> 00:01:41.557
At fixed temperature, pressure and total composition, compare its
total Gibbs energy with admissible alternatives.

02-08
00:01:41.737 --> 00:01:46.196
A fitted curve cannot, by itself, answer all three questions.

02-09
00:01:46.376 --> 00:01:50.835
A consistency score does not tell you the amount of each phase.

02-10
00:01:51.015 --> 00:01:56.668
A stability badge does not replace an explanation of the energy
comparison.

02-11
00:01:56.848 --> 00:02:00.816
Before reading a result, say which question you are asking.

02-12
00:02:00.996 --> 00:02:06.649
That sentence determines which graph, residual or material balance you
should inspect.

03-01
00:02:09.179 --> 00:02:13.745
Start with the excess Gibbs energy per mole, divided by R T.

03-02
00:02:13.925 --> 00:02:17.039
We call this dimensionless quantity g sub E.

03-03
00:02:17.219 --> 00:02:27.658
For a binary liquid, it equals x times the natural logarithm of gamma
one, plus one minus x times the natural logarithm of gamma two.

03-04
00:02:27.838 --> 00:02:30.356
Differentiate with respect to x.

03-05
00:02:30.536 --> 00:02:37.092
The product rule gives the logarithm of gamma one over gamma two, plus
two derivative terms.

03-06
00:02:37.272 --> 00:02:43.587
At fixed temperature and pressure, Gibbs-Duhem requires those last two
terms to add to zero.

03-07
00:02:43.767 --> 00:02:50.935
This is why the derivative of g sub E should equal the logarithm of
gamma one over gamma two.

03-08
00:02:51.115 --> 00:02:55.830
Our isothermal teaching example neglects the pressure contribution.

03-09
00:02:56.010 --> 00:03:02.495
For an isobaric data set whose temperature changes, an excess-enthalpy
correction is needed.

03-10
00:03:02.675 --> 00:03:07.944
We cannot apply the simplified isothermal relation indiscriminately.

03-11
00:03:08.124 --> 00:03:14.114
The path and assumptions belong beside the result, not just in the
calculation settings.

04-01
00:03:16.644 --> 00:03:24.665
For the area test, plot the natural logarithm of gamma one over gamma
two against liquid mole fraction x.

04-02
00:03:24.845 --> 00:03:27.362
Call the area above zero A plus.

04-03
00:03:27.542 --> 00:03:32.065
Call the positive magnitude of the area below zero A minus.

04-04
00:03:32.245 --> 00:03:39.270
D is one hundred times the absolute difference of these two
magnitudes, divided by their sum.

04-05
00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:47.231
In the isothermal approximation used here, the signed integral over
the full composition interval should vanish.

04-06
00:03:47.411 --> 00:03:52.851
Lab three uses the empirical criterion D less than five percent.

04-07
00:03:53.031 --> 00:03:57.276
A small D therefore indicates a small net area imbalance.

04-08
00:03:57.456 --> 00:04:01.190
It is not the percentage error of every measured point.

04-09
00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:05.871
Positive and negative departures can cancel in an integral.

04-10
00:04:06.051 --> 00:04:08.483
Also check composition coverage.

04-11
00:04:08.663 --> 00:04:14.103
If endpoints were extrapolated, the conclusion depends on that
extrapolation.

04-12
00:04:14.283 --> 00:04:23.478
If both area magnitudes vanish, the normalized ratio is undefined; do
not force a verdict from a zero denominator.

05-01
00:04:26.008 --> 00:04:30.168
The differential diagnostic asks a more local question.

05-02
00:04:30.348 --> 00:04:36.940
Subtract the logarithm of gamma one over gamma two from the derivative
of g sub E.

05-03
00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.189
The difference is r of x.

05-04
00:04:39.369 --> 00:04:45.556
Exact consistent functions give zero under the stated isothermal
approximation.

05-05
00:04:45.736 --> 00:04:53.586
A nonzero numerical residual can come from inconsistent input,
differentiation error or noise.

05-06
00:04:53.766 --> 00:05:00.358
Lab three displays one hundred times the mean absolute residual at the
interior points.

05-07
00:05:00.538 --> 00:05:03.930
Its teaching threshold is a score below five.

05-08
00:05:04.110 --> 00:05:06.180
This score is dimensionless.

05-09
00:05:06.360 --> 00:05:14.232
Multiplying by one hundred does not make it a percentage error,
because there is no relative-error denominator.

05-10
00:05:14.412 --> 00:05:18.721
Notice the distinction between the local curve and its average.

05-11
00:05:18.901 --> 00:05:22.720
A narrow peak can remain even when the average is small.

05-12
00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:24.052
Read both.

05-13
00:05:24.232 --> 00:05:28.477
A pass means that this selected summary met its criterion.

05-14
00:05:28.657 --> 00:05:33.436
It does not prove that every point exactly satisfies Gibbs-Duhem.

06-01
00:05:35.966 --> 00:05:39.038
Here is the example from the opening reading.

06-02
00:05:39.218 --> 00:05:50.489
Use the synthetic isothermal data at three hundred and fifty kelvin, a
localized defect with amplitude zero point two five, and eighty-one
sampling points.

06-03
00:05:50.669 --> 00:05:54.189
D is about one point nine five six percent.

06-04
00:05:54.369 --> 00:05:58.550
The differential score is about one point five nine one.

06-05
00:05:58.730 --> 00:06:00.352
Both summaries pass.

06-06
00:06:00.532 --> 00:06:08.254
Yet the residual near x equal to zero point three five is about
negative zero point two five zero.

06-07
00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.461
The local departure remains.

06-08
00:06:10.641 --> 00:06:17.020
Changing from forty-one to one hundred and sixty-one points changes D
only slightly.

06-09
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:23.301
These are denser samples of the same synthetic function, not new
experimental measurements.

06-10
00:06:23.481 --> 00:06:29.369
More points can improve the numerical approximation and reveal the
shape of the defect.

06-11
00:06:29.549 --> 00:06:32.664
They cannot remove the defect from the function.

06-12
00:06:32.844 --> 00:06:40.204
Nor does this comparison establish one universally sufficient number
of experimental data points.

06-13
00:06:40.384 --> 00:06:44.458
Inspect the residual curve before writing your conclusion.

07-01
00:06:46.988 --> 00:06:54.434
The Van Ness approach examines modeling capability within
thermodynamic consistency testing.

07-02
00:06:54.614 --> 00:06:57.259
Our Lab uses a teaching variant.

07-03
00:06:57.439 --> 00:07:08.127
It fits a consistent N R T L activity model jointly to pressure and
vapor composition, at the observed temperature and liquid composition.

07-04
00:07:08.307 --> 00:07:14.558
The pressure metric is the mean absolute relative difference,
multiplied by one hundred.

07-05
00:07:14.738 --> 00:07:22.674
The vapor-composition metric is the mean absolute mole-fraction
difference, also multiplied by one hundred.

07-06
00:07:22.854 --> 00:07:24.880
Their denominators differ.

07-07
00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:32.399
The criteria are pressure error below one percent, and vapor-
composition difference below one percentage point.

07-08
00:07:32.579 --> 00:07:36.483
The second is not a relative percentage error in y.

07-09
00:07:36.663 --> 00:07:43.020
Meeting both limits means that this model reproduces these
observations under these assumptions.

07-10
00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.846
It does not certify each measurement.

07-11
00:07:46.026 --> 00:07:52.127
Missing a limit can reflect the model, property correlations or
experimental data.

07-12
00:07:52.307 --> 00:07:56.254
This teaching fit is not the full NIST implementation.

07-13
00:07:56.434 --> 00:08:00.530
Report the method and its assumptions along with the two numbers.

08-01
00:08:03.060 --> 00:08:05.300
Now turn to phase stability.

08-02
00:08:05.480 --> 00:08:07.997
The vertical axis changes meaning.

08-03
00:08:08.177 --> 00:08:13.691
Here, g is the total mixing Gibbs energy divided by n R T.

08-04
00:08:13.871 --> 00:08:18.052
It includes ideal mixing and the excess contribution.

08-05
00:08:18.232 --> 00:08:24.099
It is not the excess-only quantity g sub E from the consistency
calculation.

08-06
00:08:24.279 --> 00:08:27.991
For the symmetric Margules example, A is three.

08-07
00:08:28.171 --> 00:08:34.699
At each possible composition x, the curve gives the energy of one
homogeneous liquid.

08-08
00:08:34.879 --> 00:08:38.996
The symbol z denotes our overall feed composition.

08-09
00:08:39.176 --> 00:08:46.771
We compare a homogeneous liquid at z with possible splits that
conserve the same overall composition.

08-10
00:08:46.951 --> 00:08:51.986
Keep temperature, pressure and the reference state fixed during that
comparison.

08-11
00:08:52.166 --> 00:08:55.984
The horizontal axis is composition, not time.

08-12
00:08:56.164 --> 00:09:02.863
Moving along the curve does not show a droplet evolving or a
separation process proceeding.

08-13
00:09:03.043 --> 00:09:08.376
The graph describes possible states and their energies within this
liquid model.

09-01
00:09:10.906 --> 00:09:13.061
Follow the labels on the graph.

09-02
00:09:13.241 --> 00:09:16.846
The amber curve represents the homogeneous liquid.

09-03
00:09:17.026 --> 00:09:26.008
The two contact compositions are about zero point zero seven zero
seven two and zero point nine two nine two eight.

09-04
00:09:26.188 --> 00:09:32.929
These are the compositions inside the candidate coexisting phases, not
their relative amounts.

09-05
00:09:33.109 --> 00:09:36.565
The graphite common tangent touches both contacts.

09-06
00:09:36.745 --> 00:09:44.510
It is horizontal here because this example is symmetric; a common
tangent is not always horizontal.

09-07
00:09:44.690 --> 00:09:51.176
The dashed teal equilibrium envelope follows the supported two-liquid
line between the contacts.

09-08
00:09:51.356 --> 00:09:55.537
Now read vertically at z equal to zero point one two.

09-09
00:09:55.717 --> 00:09:59.194
The amber diamond marks the homogeneous feed.

09-10
00:09:59.374 --> 00:10:05.668
The teal circle below it marks the two-liquid state with the same
overall composition.

09-11
00:10:05.848 --> 00:10:10.072
The difference in height is the Gibbs energy decrease on splitting.

09-12
00:10:10.252 --> 00:10:18.337
Always compare the two heights at the same overall composition, rather
than comparing arbitrary points along the curve.

10-01
00:10:20.867 --> 00:10:24.878
First check the curvature of the homogeneous curve at the feed.

10-02
00:10:25.058 --> 00:10:32.610
For this model, the second derivative is one over x, plus one over one
minus x, minus twice A.

10-03
00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:42.859
At x equal to zero point one two, substitute one over zero point one
two, plus one over zero point eight eight, minus six.

10-04
00:10:43.039 --> 00:10:47.284
The result is about positive three point four six nine seven.

10-05
00:10:47.464 --> 00:10:55.315
Positive curvature means that the homogeneous liquid resists
infinitesimal composition fluctuations.

10-06
00:10:55.495 --> 00:11:00.722
It does not exclude a lower-energy split into substantially different
compositions.

10-07
00:11:00.902 --> 00:11:06.320
Negative curvature identifies a locally unstable homogeneous state.

10-08
00:11:06.500 --> 00:11:10.042
Zero curvature needs further examination.

10-09
00:11:10.222 --> 00:11:18.776
To distinguish a globally stable state from a metastable one, we still
need an energy comparison with admissible alternatives.

10-10
00:11:18.956 --> 00:11:22.412
Do not stop at the positive sign or at a badge.

10-11
00:11:22.592 --> 00:11:27.947
Write what was tested: local curvature, at the specified feed
composition.

11-01
00:11:30.477 --> 00:11:36.322
Use the contact compositions to construct a split with the correct
material balance.

11-02
00:11:36.502 --> 00:11:44.012
The beta phase fraction equals z minus x alpha, divided by x beta
minus x alpha.

11-03
00:11:44.192 --> 00:11:51.637
At z equal to zero point one two, substitution gives zero point zero
five seven three nine eight.

11-04
00:11:51.817 --> 00:11:58.438
The alpha fraction is one minus that value, or zero point nine four
two six zero two.

11-05
00:11:58.618 --> 00:12:03.204
These are mole fractions of the total amount assigned to each phase.

11-06
00:12:03.384 --> 00:12:07.502
Their weighted composition returns zero point one two.

11-07
00:12:07.682 --> 00:12:10.013
Next, evaluate the energy.

11-08
00:12:10.193 --> 00:12:16.636
The homogeneous value is about negative zero point zero five zero one
two five.

11-09
00:12:16.816 --> 00:12:23.109
The weighted split value is about negative zero point zero five eight
three four one.

11-10
00:12:23.289 --> 00:12:30.798
The positive difference shown on the slide is the Gibbs energy
reduction, measured in R T per mole of feed.

11-11
00:12:30.978 --> 00:12:37.869
Positive local curvature with a lower admissible split identifies this
feed as metastable.

12-01
00:12:40.399 --> 00:12:44.132
A lower split rejects global stability of the feed.

12-02
00:12:44.312 --> 00:12:52.206
To establish that the chosen split is the equilibrium state, check
that its common tangent supports the whole curve.

12-03
00:12:52.386 --> 00:13:00.044
The curve minus that line must be nonnegative at every admissible
composition, with equality at the contacts.

12-04
00:13:00.224 --> 00:13:05.686
In Lab four, the common-tangent residual checks the coexistence
equations.

12-05
00:13:05.866 --> 00:13:08.298
It does not check global support.

12-06
00:13:08.478 --> 00:13:14.430
The feed tangent-plane-distance view uses the tangent at the
homogeneous feed.

12-07
00:13:14.610 --> 00:13:19.495
A negative value somewhere rejects global stability of that feed.

12-08
00:13:19.675 --> 00:13:21.467
These are different checks.

12-09
00:13:21.647 --> 00:13:25.402
Finally, change only z to zero point three five.

12-10
00:13:25.582 --> 00:13:31.191
At the same temperature, pressure and model, the contact compositions
stay fixed.

12-11
00:13:31.371 --> 00:13:36.363
The beta fraction becomes about zero point three two five two eight
nine.

12-12
00:13:36.543 --> 00:13:41.172
The relative amounts change, and the feed curvature is now negative.

12-13
00:13:41.352 --> 00:13:48.014
Equilibrium selects coexistence compositions; material balance selects
the phase amounts.

13-01
00:13:50.544 --> 00:13:55.621
Pause for about four minutes and write R one through R three in your
notebook.

13-02
00:13:55.801 --> 00:14:03.179
For R one, both D and the mean residual score pass, but the residual
curve has a narrow peak.

13-03
00:14:03.359 --> 00:14:07.138
Can you certify every point, and what should you inspect?

13-04
00:14:07.318 --> 00:14:11.243
For R two, A is three and z is zero point one two.

13-05
00:14:11.423 --> 00:14:16.390
Curvature is positive, but a supported split has lower Gibbs energy.

13-06
00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:20.261
Classify the homogeneous feed and explain why.

13-07
00:14:20.441 --> 00:14:26.286
For R three, move z to zero point three five on the same supported tie
line.

13-08
00:14:26.466 --> 00:14:35.057
State what stays fixed, calculate the beta phase fraction, and explain
why Week four still needs material balances.

13-09
00:14:35.237 --> 00:14:38.949
Keep your first answers, then continue to the check.

14-01
00:14:41.479 --> 00:14:43.989
For R one, the answer is no.

14-02
00:14:44.169 --> 00:14:50.527
Integrated or averaged summaries can hide cancellation and localized
departures.

14-03
00:14:50.707 --> 00:14:57.171
Inspect the residual shape, assumptions, composition coverage and
numerical sensitivity.

14-04
00:14:57.351 --> 00:14:59.548
State which criterion passed.

14-05
00:14:59.728 --> 00:15:03.547
For R two, the homogeneous feed is metastable.

14-06
00:15:03.727 --> 00:15:10.741
Its positive curvature gives local resistance, but the supported split
has lower Gibbs energy.

14-07
00:15:10.921 --> 00:15:14.975
For R three, the two contact compositions stay fixed.

14-08
00:15:15.155 --> 00:15:26.269
The lever rule gives a beta fraction of zero point three two five two
eight nine and an alpha fraction of zero point six seven four seven
one one.

14-09
00:15:26.449 --> 00:15:31.164
Equilibrium conditions do not replace inventory balances.

14-10
00:15:31.344 --> 00:15:39.579
That distinction carries directly into Week four, where we must check
whether enough material remains for a solid phase to exist.

14-11
00:15:39.759 --> 00:15:42.745
Add corrections beside your first answers.

14-12
00:15:42.925 --> 00:15:47.512
Now continue with the six-page worksheet and four-page Lab guide.

14-13
00:15:47.692 --> 00:15:51.617
